sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: swamp/workflows/workflow-operation-task-vm-provision.yaml (+ task-vm-provision, provider-incus, incus-first-visit, enrollment, ssh-prepare, model-issue, runtime children), docs/reference/compute-drivers.md, docs/reference/task-vm-native-provision.md, docs/reference/tasks.md, DECISIONS R1-R4.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 14, 15, 16, 18, 19, 20, 22 (provider, first boot, bootstrap and peer steps merged; no input-check, decode or report steps; cfgmgmt on the pinned guest; one end check).
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · task VM claim for a task group
  %% kind: task_vm.provision
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane H icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane VM icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  %% lane HUB icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant H as Task VM host<br/>Node 13 over ssh
  participant VM as Task VM<br/>tvm-333 over ssh
  participant HUB as VPN hub<br/>reached over ssh
  Note over GW: Validate, record the task<br/>task VM 333 · task #5181 · Node 13
  %%> Started by:
  %%> - `AllocateTaskVmAction`, when a task group claims a VM; there is no CLI command
  %%> - an owning Task parent captures the same intent and runs these steps in its own run
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - task VM 333 is `provisioning`, with its Placement on Node 13 and the promoted recipe image
  %%> - no other task holds `task_vm:333`; a repeated admission returns the same open task
  %%> Result:
  %%> - task #5181 records the full intent: provider, host, image, size, guest Node 340, hub peer file, observer files, Pi Process and model key record
  %%> - vault copies for the model key, Pi token, Pi models and provider bootstrap; the intent holds only their vault key names
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-task-vm-provision
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - `operation_id`, `node_id`, `kind`, the intent and `execution_secrets` as vault key names
  %%> - the input schema validates the intent; no `eligible` assert or `provider-plan` decode step re-checks it
  SW->>H: Create and start the VM<br/>incus · @swamp/ssh exec
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one `incus launch` argv on Node 13 creates and starts `tvm-333` from the admitted image, with its metadata and the read-only package cache
  %%> - a `guard` skips it when `tvm-333` already runs the admitted image, so a resumed run allocates no second VM
  %%> - a topology group clones its seed volume in the same step
  %%> Per provider:
  %%> - UpCloud overflow: the `cloud` step creates the labelled server through the API from 209, with the token from the vault
  %%> Replaces:
  %%> - `observe-vm`, `plan`, `observe-seed`, `seed-plan`, `clone-seed`, `seed-ready`, `initialize-vm`, `read-metadata`, `metadata`, `write-metadata`, `start-vm`, `inspect-created-vm` and `provider`
  SW->>H: Wait for first boot<br/>incus-first-visit · agent, host key
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one bounded argv: `incus wait tvm-333 agent --timeout 120`, then `cloud-init status --wait`, then prints the guest's public SSH host key
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the first wait is `incus wait tvm-333 agent --timeout 120`, as the slot start does: `cloud-init status --wait` runs through `incus exec`, which fails at once while the VM agent is not running (enrol 12264)
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the provider observation is the trusted source of the guest's host key
  %%> Replaces:
  %%> - `guest-available`, `cloud-init`, `guest-key` and the `guest` decode step
  SW->>SW: Pin the guest host key<br/>vm-known-hosts · 209 known_hosts
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - replaces any entry for the guest's address in the 209 worker's `known_hosts` with the observed key
  %%> Why:
  %%> - official cfgmgmt takes no pinned key and task VMs reuse addresses; without this, a stale entry fails every cfgmgmt step
  %%> Held open:
  %%> - a 209 shell step until cfgmgmt accepts a pinned host key
  SW->>H: Bootstrap the guest<br/>bootstrap · incus exec
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - through `incus exec` on Node 13: the managed account, 209's authorized key, passwordless sudo, sshd with TCP 22 only from 209's WireGuard address, the firewall rules that also let 209 and the Gateway reach Pi on TCP 3774, and a new WireGuard key whose public half it prints
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the same firewall step allows TCP 443 on the `orbit` interface (`orbit:task-vm-site`), and the hub's task VM filter lets 209 in on 443 (`orbit:task-vm-control-web`) beside its SSH rule, so the Cluster router and 209's TLS probe reach a routed workspace; Pi and SSH stay limited to 209 and the Gateway, so the Node's full WireGuard member trust is not applied
  %%> - each phase is one argv the Gateway rendered; no step reads bootstrap assets
  %%> Replaces:
  %%> - the five `bootstrap-*` exec and `-assets` pairs, `peer-key-command`, `peer-key` and the `ssh-prepare` child
  %%> - `package`: the recipe image ships `openssh-server` (rule 2)
  SW->>HUB: Add the hub peer<br/>hub-peer · cfgmgmt file · addconf
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - `@swamp/cfgmgmt/file` writes `orbit-peers.d/340.conf` on the hub, then `wg addconf orbit` loads only that peer
  %%> Why:
  %%> - no other peer changes, so overlapping enrollments never drop each other
  SW->>H: Start the guest tunnel<br/>peer · incus exec · wg-quick
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - writes the guest's `orbit.conf` and DNS marker, then `systemctl enable --now wg-quick@orbit`
  %%> - from here Swamp reaches tvm-333 directly over WireGuard
  %%> Replaces:
  %%> - `peer-publish-command`, `peer-enable`, `peer-start` and `peer-dns-command`
  SW->>SW: Issue the model key<br/>model · task-vm-model-issue
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - adds VM 333's model key on the model router from 209 and confirms the anchor key stays
  %%> - two Ansible phases on 209, `anchor` and `issue`: HTTPS calls to the router, no script
  %%> When:
  %%> - in parallel from the start of the run; it touches no guest (rule 18)
  %%> Held open:
  %%> - one 209 script until an official HTTP behavior covers the router API; seven steps become one
  SW->>VM: Start the Node agent<br/>observer · cfgmgmt file · systemd
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - `@swamp/cfgmgmt/file` writes the agent identity, `config.toml` and `ca.pem`; `@swamp/cfgmgmt/systemd` installs and starts `orbit-agent`
  %%> - today the steps are `observer-group` (one `groupadd -f --system orbit-env` argv, since the unit names that group) and `observer-unit`
  %%> - `observer-identity-create` makes the secret with `openssl rand -hex`, and `observer-identity-trim` cuts the file to its 64 characters, because the agent trims the file and the Gateway checks `sha256` of the trimmed value; `observer-identity` records that digest
  %%> - the Gateway makes the agent secret at admission, as for `node:add`, and keeps its hash; `observer-identity-create` writes the vault copy `agent-secret-node-<id>` on stdin of one `install` (0600, no newline), and `observer-identity` proves the file hashes to that value
  %%> - runs beside the Pi install
  %%> Replaces:
  %%> - `observer-identity`, `observer-configuration`, `observer-ca`, `observer-unit`, `observer-reload` and `observer-start` (inline Python writes)
  SW->>VM: Install the Pi server<br/>runtime · Ansible phase install
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - fetches the pinned Pi build to 209 and checks its digest there
  %%> - Ansible phase `install`, over SSH with the pinned guest host key: copies the build (its digest is checked on the staged file first), writes the Orbit root (under the image's name `orbit-root.crt` and the fleet's `orbit-managed-root-ca.crt`), the Pi models and the Pi token (0600, from `vault.get` through the environment, `no_log`), installs the unit, and restarts `pi-server` when a file changed
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the fleet's name is the file a Route writes; with it in place, the first Route on the VM keeps the CA store (op 12259 rebuilt it in 2.3 s).
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the phase also creates the app-dev runtime folders and tmpfiles file (`AppDevRuntimeFootprint`, the same list the app-dev role add applies), because the VM records the app-dev role and a served workspace site writes its hibernation log to `/data/caddy/orbit/hibernation`; a ready slot gets them at its enrol, and slots enrolled before are replaced through the pool (its setting cleared, then set again)
  %%> - Ansible phase `check`: the service is active and `/capabilities` answers 200 to the bearer
  %%> - the run folder, with the staged build, is removed in a last job
  %%> - not `@swamp/cfgmgmt`: it cannot pin a host key toward a task VM
  %%> Replaces:
  %%> - the eleven `task-vm-runtime` guest steps; its `result` step stays, because enrollment reads it
  SW->>VM: Check the guest is ready<br/>check · agent, Pi, private ssh
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one pinned exec over WireGuard collects `orbit-agent` and `pi-server` state and the Pi capabilities on the bind address; Swamp's `assert` checks them
  %%> Replaces:
  %%> - `verify`, `Check the Pi service`, `Probe the Pi server`, `receipt`, both `result` steps and the root `result` print (rules 9, 14)
  alt every step passes
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>tvm-333 ready · Node 340
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its run reader sees the terminal event and reads the `check` step's outputs
    %%> Result:
    %%> - records the guest host key with `RecordNodeSshHostKeyAction`, the WireGuard key, observer identity and the `pi-server` Process
    %%> - task VM 333 is `ready`; task #5181 succeeds
  else a step fails
    SW->>SW: Revoke the model key<br/>model-revoke · on failure
    %%> When:
    %%> - `dependsOn` the failed step with condition `failed` (rule 19)
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - revokes any issued model key; for UpCloud, `cloud-prefix-rollback` also deletes the unready server
    %%> On the Node:
    %%> - the Incus VM and a published hub peer stay for diagnosis; the next hub convergence drops the peer
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    %%> Result:
    %%> - task #5181 fails; task VM 333 is `failed` with the error and keeps its cleanup intent
    %%> - the group asks for assistance; `tasks:cancel` destroys the VM
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - the guards skip finished work on a resumed run
  end
